-- Updates questions whose content changed after they were first seeded into Supabase.
-- Unlike seed.sql (on conflict do nothing, insert-only), this UPDATEs by id since these
-- rows already exist in the questions table. Safe to run once; re-running is harmless
-- (idempotent — it just re-applies the same current data).

update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-15","rotationIndex":15,"stem":"Regarding units and measurement of ionising radiation:","choices":["The gray (Gy) is the SI unit of absorbed dose, defined as one joule of energy absorbed per kilogram of tissue","The sievert (Sv) expresses absorbed dose to tissue without any adjustment for the type of radiation involved","The becquerel (Bq) is a measure of radioactivity, defined as one nuclear disintegration per second","Effective dose takes into account the differing radiosensitivity of the various organs and tissues exposed","The roentgen is the current SI unit used to quantify a patient''s ionising radiation exposure in clinical practice"],"correct":[true,false,true,true,false],"explanation":"<p><strong>A. True.</strong> The Gray is defined exactly this way (J/kg) and measures absorbed dose only, with no biological weighting.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. False.</strong> The Sievert is used for equivalent and effective dose, which are derived from absorbed dose by applying weighting factors for radiation type and tissue sensitivity — it is not a raw, unadjusted measure.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. True.</strong> This is the standard definition of the Becquerel, replacing the older Curie.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. True.</strong> This is precisely what distinguishes effective dose from equivalent dose — it sums weighted equivalent doses across all exposed organs according to their relative radiosensitivity.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. False.</strong> The roentgen is an older, non-SI unit of exposure (ionisation in air). The SI unit of exposure is the coulomb per kilogram (C/kg); clinical dose reporting now uses Gray and Sievert rather than the roentgen.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Radiation Dose Quantities</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><p style=\"margin:0\">When ionising radiation interacts with the body, several distinct quantities are used to describe it, and confusing them is a common source of error. Absorbed dose (Gray) simply measures energy deposited per unit mass, regardless of the biological effect that energy produces. To account for the fact that different types of radiation (alpha, beta, X-ray, neutron) cause different amounts of biological damage for the same absorbed dose, a radiation weighting factor is applied to give the equivalent dose, measured in Sieverts. X-rays have a weighting factor of 1, so 1 gray = 1 Sievert. A further tissue weighting factor (reflecting how radiosensitive a given organ is) gives the effective dose — also expressed in Sieverts. Activity, the rate at which a radioactive source decays, is a separate concept measured in Becquerels and is unrelated to the dose received by a person nearby.</p></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_radiation_units_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-16","rotationIndex":19,"stem":"When exposed to ionizing radiation:","choices":["Nerve cells are more vulnerable than haemopoietic cells","Gut cells are less vulnerable than haemopoietic cells","Gastrointestinal symptoms are immediate when the dose is 0.5 Gy","Death occurs in 100% of cases within 2 weeks after exposure to 10–20 Gy","Death occurs due to central nervous system failure after exposure to at least 20 Gy"],"correct":[false,true,false,true,true],"explanation":"<p><strong>A. False.</strong> The cells most vulnerable to radiation are those with the greatest rate of replication and least differentiation — the Law of Bergonié and Tribondeau. Haemopoietic precursors divide rapidly and are highly radiosensitive, whereas nerve cells are post-mitotic and among the least radiosensitive tissues in the body.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. True.</strong> Gut epithelial cells do divide actively, but haemopoietic precursors divide even more rapidly and are more susceptible to radiation-induced apoptosis. The hierarchy from most to least radiosensitive: haemopoietic → reproductive → gastrointestinal → epidermis/eyes → other organs → support tissues → nerve.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. False.</strong> Immediate gastrointestinal symptoms appear at doses above 1.5 Gy, not at 0.5 Gy. At 0.5 Gy, only blood count changes are expected. Nausea, vomiting, and fatigue are likely at 0.5–1.5 Gy but become immediate only once the dose exceeds 1.5 Gy.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. True.</strong> Whole-body exposure to 10–20 Gy is uniformly fatal within two weeks. The cause of death is gastrointestinal tract failure — ablation of the rapidly dividing intestinal epithelium leads to mucosal sloughing, fluid loss, bacterial translocation, and overwhelming sepsis.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. True.</strong> Exposure to more than 20 Gy causes death due to central nervous system failure. Despite nerves being comparatively radioresistant, extreme doses cause overwhelming vascular injury, cerebral oedema, and neuronal death, typically within hours to a few days.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Whole-Body Dose &amp; Radiosensitivity</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><table style=\"margin:0\"><thead><tr><th>Absorbed Dose (Gy)</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&lt; 0.05</td><td>No immediate effect</td></tr><tr><td>0.05–0.5</td><td>Change in blood count</td></tr><tr><td>0.5–1.5</td><td>Change in blood count; likely nausea, vomiting, fatigue, and loss of appetite</td></tr><tr><td>1.5–10</td><td>GI symptoms appear immediately; severe changes in blood; 1.5 Gy is the death threshold (most sensitive individuals); 3–5 Gy — 50% die within 60 days without intensive support; death due to bone marrow suppression, requiring transplant to recover</td></tr><tr><td>10–20</td><td>Death in 100% within 2 weeks; death due to GI failure</td></tr><tr><td>&gt; 20</td><td>Death due to CNS failure</td></tr></tbody></table><img src=\"images/radiosensitivity-pyramid.png\" alt=\"Radiosensitivity pyramid diagram — Bergonié and Tribondeau law showing tissue radiosensitivity from highest (haematopoietic) to lowest (nerve), correlated with rate of cell division\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;margin-top:16px;border:1px solid #EDE3D7\"></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_radiation_cell_vulnerability_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-16","rotationIndex":22,"question":"Under the Ionising Radiation (Medical Exposure) Regulations (IRMER), who holds legal responsibility for justifying a fluoro-guided medical exposure before it goes ahead?","choices":["Radiation Protection Supervisor","Referrer","Operator","Practitioner","Employer"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<p><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> The Radiation Protection Supervisor (RPS) is a role defined under the Ionising Radiation Regulations (IRR), not IRMER. The RPS oversees day-to-day compliance with staff and public radiation safety in designated areas — this is an occupational safety role, not a patient-exposure role.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> The referrer requests the exposure and provides the clinical information that justifies the need for the investigation. However, the referrer does not hold the legal responsibility for authorising the individual exposure — that duty is assigned specifically to the practitioner.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> The operator is whoever practically carries out any part of the exposure — for example, pressing the fluoroscopy pedal, positioning the patient, or administering contrast. Operators must be adequately trained but do not bear the legal responsibility for justification.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D. Correct.</strong> Under IRMER, the <strong>practitioner</strong> is the person legally accountable for justifying that a specific medical exposure is appropriate for the individual patient before it is carried out. The practitioner must be entitled to act in this role under their employer''s written procedures.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> The employer''s duty under IRMER is to establish the systems, protocols, and training that enable compliance across the organisation — for example, drawing up written procedures and ensuring adequate equipment. The employer does not justify individual patient exposures.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">IRMER vs IRR</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><p style=\"margin:0\">IRMER (Ionising Radiation [Medical Exposure] Regulations) and the Ionising Radiation Regulations (IRR) are frequently confused because they both concern ionising radiation, but they protect different people and define different duties. IRR protects staff and the public from occupational and environmental radiation exposure: it covers designating controlled and supervised areas, appointing a Radiation Protection Adviser (RPA) and Radiation Protection Supervisor (RPS), and setting dose limits for workers. IRMER, by contrast, protects the patient undergoing a medical exposure and defines four key duty-holder roles: the <strong>referrer</strong> (supplies the clinical justification for requesting the exposure), the <strong>practitioner</strong> (legally responsible for justifying each individual exposure), the <strong>operator</strong> (practically carries out the exposure), and the <strong>employer</strong> (establishes the organisational framework of procedures and training).</p></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_irmer_justification_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-23","rotationIndex":26,"stem":"With regards to peripheral nerve injury:","choices":["Neurapraxia is characterised by disruption of the axon with preservation of the endoneurial scaffold.","Sunderland Grade III injury involves axonal and endoneurial disruption with preservation of the perineurium and epineurium.","Wallerian degeneration is typically well established within 1–2 weeks of a neurotmetic injury.","Neurapraxia is associated with Wallerian degeneration and recovery occurring over several months.","Sunderland Grade V injury is synonymous with neurotmesis, involving complete disruption of both axons and the scaffold."],"correct":[false,true,true,false,true],"explanation":"<p>A traumatic peripheral nerve injury can be graded by Seddon''s classification (neurapraxia, axonotmesis, neurotmesis) or Sunderland''s five-grade system, which predicts prognosis and the need for surgery.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A – False:</strong> Neurapraxia is a myelin injury only; the axon and scaffold remain intact and Wallerian degeneration does not occur.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B – True:</strong> Sunderland Grade III involves axonal and endoneurial disruption but the perineurium and epineurium remain preserved.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C – True:</strong> Wallerian degeneration begins within days and is well established by 1–2 weeks after axonal injury.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D – False:</strong> Neurapraxia recovers rapidly (3–6 weeks) via remyelination, without Wallerian degeneration.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E – True:</strong> Grade V is complete neurotmesis — disruption of both axons and the entire scaffold.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Seddon &amp; Sunderland Classification</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><div style=\"text-align:center\"><img src=\"images/seddon_sunderland_classification.png\" alt=\"Seddon-Sunderland classification table comparing nerve injury grades\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;margin-top:0;border:1px solid #EDE3D7\"></div></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_peripheral_nerve_injury_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-07-23","rotationIndex":27,"stem":"Regarding the cellular structure of a peripheral nerve:","choices":["The endoneurium is the outermost layer of the nerve and confers its tensile strength.","Groups of axons are organised into fascicles, each surrounded by the perineurium.","The basal lamina surrounding each axon is secreted by Schwann cells.","Schwann cells retain their repair phenotype indefinitely, regardless of any delay to reinnervation.","Peripheral nerves receive their blood supply from a single dominant vascular pedicle at each level."],"correct":[false,true,true,false,false],"explanation":"<p>Peripheral nerve structure is organised through a central nervous tissue (axon) which conducts electrical signal. This is wrapped around by myelin from glial cells which speeds signal transmission. On the outer layer of the glial cells is a layer called the basal lamina which is made of proteins. These are then surrounded by layers of connective tissue from inside to the outside as follows: endoneurium → fascicle → perineurium → epineurium.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A – False:</strong> The epineurium is the outermost layer and provides tensile strength; the endoneurium surrounds individual axons.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>B – True:</strong> Fascicles (groups of axons) are each wrapped in the multilayered perineurium.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>C – True:</strong> The basal lamina is an extracellular matrix secreted by Schwann cells, maintaining axon–Schwann cell alignment.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>D – False:</strong> If reinnervation is delayed beyond 6–12 weeks, Schwann cells progressively lose their repair phenotype, with some undergoing apoptosis.</p><p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>E – False:</strong> Nerves are supplied by widely separated segmental pedicles feeding longitudinal vascular plexi, not a single pedicle.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Peripheral Nerve Structure</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><div style=\"text-align:center\"><img src=\"images/axon-image.png\" alt=\"Diagram of peripheral nerve cellular structure showing axon, Schwann cell, basal lamina, endoneurium, fascicle, perineurium, and epineurium\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;margin-top:0;border:1px solid #EDE3D7\"></div></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_peripheral_nerve_structure_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-08-10","rotationIndex":39,"question":"A 58-year-old man taking rivaroxaban 20 mg once daily for atrial fibrillation, with normal renal function, is due to have a cervical transforaminal nerve root block for cervical radicular pain. What is the most appropriate management of his rivaroxaban before the procedure?","choices":["Continue rivaroxaban unchanged","Discontinue rivaroxaban 3 days before the procedure","Discontinue rivaroxaban 5 days before the procedure","Discontinue rivaroxaban 7 days before the procedure","Bridge with therapeutic-dose LMWH after stopping rivaroxaban"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<p>Cervical transforaminal nerve root blocks sit in the high-risk category because of the proximity to the vertebral artery and spinal cord, so anticoagulation must be interrupted rather than continued.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Continuing a DOAC through a high-risk procedure is not acceptable.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Correct.</strong> DOACs are renally cleared and should be stopped for 5 half-lives before high-risk procedures. For rivaroxaban with normal renal function, this equates to 3 days (the exception being severe renal impairment &mdash; CrCl &lt;15 ml/min &mdash; where clearance becomes unpredictable).</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C, D. Incorrect.</strong> These durations are excessive for a DOAC with normal renal clearance and unnecessarily prolong the patient''s time off anticoagulation. Dabigatran is more reliant on renal clearance and is therefore stopped for 4 days rather than 3 (reflecting its longer half-life) &mdash; this does not apply to rivaroxaban.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Bridging is not routinely required for DOACs. In cases of very high thrombotic risk, this should be assessed on an individual basis with the haematology team.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Procedural Risk Stratification</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><table style=\"margin:0 0 6px\">\n    <tr><th>Risk Category</th><th>Example Procedures</th></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\"><td style=\"color:var(--wrong);font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">High</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Spinal cord stimulation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Intrathecal drug delivery systems</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Cervical transforaminal epidurals / nerve root blocks</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\"><td style=\"color:#7A5200;font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">Intermediate</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Interlaminar epidurals</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Thoracic &amp; lumbar transforaminal epidurals / nerve root blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Cervical facet medial branch blocks &amp; RF denervation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Sympathetic blocks (stellate, thoracic, splanchnic, coeliac, lumbar, hypogastric)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve stimulation trial &amp; implant (location-dependent)</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\"><td style=\"color:var(--correct);font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">Low</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral joint &amp; musculoskeletal injections</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Trigger point injections (incl. piriformis)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Sacroiliac joint injection &amp; sacral lateral branch blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Thoracic &amp; lumbar facet medial branch blocks &amp; RF denervation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve stimulation trial &amp; implant (location-dependent)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Pocket revision &amp; IPG placement</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--stone);margin:2px 0 16px\">In patients with elevated bleeding risk, reclassify intermediate-risk procedures as high risk and low-risk procedures as intermediate.</div>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--correct);margin-bottom:8px\">Low Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0 0 16px\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>Continue in most situations</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>Continue in most situations</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>Continue &mdash; ensure last dose taken &ge;6 hours before the procedure. Individual assessment if bleeding risk is high or INR &gt;3.0.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>Omit on day of procedure &mdash; allow &ge;2 half-lives since last dose (usually 24 hours). Longer if CrCl &lt;15 (drug accumulates unpredictably).</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>As other DOACs</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>48 hours</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A5200;margin-bottom:8px\">Intermediate Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0 0 16px\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Consider stopping if anatomically higher bleeding risk (e.g. cervical epidural, stellate ganglion block)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>7 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>5 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>5 days (target INR &le;1.2). Consider LMWH bridging if high thrombotic risk.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>3 days (5 half-lives)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>4 days (6 days if renal impairment)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--wrong);margin-bottom:8px\">High Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Primary prevention: 6 days<br>Secondary prevention: 4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>7 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>5 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>48 hours</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>5 days (target INR &le;1.2). Consider LMWH bridging if high thrombotic risk.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>3 days (5 half-lives)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>4 days (6 days if renal impairment)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_anticoag_rivaroxaban_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-08-10","rotationIndex":40,"question":"A 70-year-old woman on warfarin for a mechanical aortic valve requires an elective intrathecal drug delivery system implant. Her INR is 3.4 (target range 2.5–3.5). What is the most appropriate perioperative anticoagulation strategy?","choices":["Continue warfarin unchanged throughout","Stop warfarin with no bridging, restart afterwards","Stop warfarin, bridge with therapeutic-dose LMWH, restart warfarin post-procedure","Switch to a DOAC for the perioperative period","Reduce the warfarin dose without stopping, to lower the INR to 2.0"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<p>Intrathecal pump implantation is a high-risk procedure requiring an INR &le;1.2, but this patient''s mechanical valve places her in the high thromboembolic-risk category.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> An INR of 3.4 is unsafe for a high-risk neuraxial-adjacent implant.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Leaving a mechanical valve patient unprotected during the anticoagulant-free window risks valve thrombosis.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Correct.</strong> Bridging with a form of heparin is reserved for patients like this, whose thromboembolic risk without anticoagulation is high &mdash; distinct from routine AF, where the BRIDGE trial showed bridging is unhelpful. Discussion with the local team is warranted to decide whether therapeutic-dose LMWH (stopped 24 hours pre-procedure) is safe, or whether admission for IV heparin (stopped 6 hours pre-procedure) is necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Mechanical valves are not a licensed indication for DOACs, which have shown inferior outcomes in this population.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> An INR of 2.0 is still too high for a high-risk procedure and does not address the interruption/bridging question.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Procedural Risk Stratification</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><table style=\"margin:0 0 6px\">\n    <tr><th>Risk Category</th><th>Example Procedures</th></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\"><td style=\"color:var(--wrong);font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">High</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Spinal cord stimulation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Intrathecal drug delivery systems</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Cervical transforaminal epidurals / nerve root blocks</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\"><td style=\"color:#7A5200;font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">Intermediate</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Interlaminar epidurals</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Thoracic &amp; lumbar transforaminal epidurals / nerve root blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Cervical facet medial branch blocks &amp; RF denervation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Sympathetic blocks (stellate, thoracic, splanchnic, coeliac, lumbar, hypogastric)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve stimulation trial &amp; implant (location-dependent)</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\"><td style=\"color:var(--correct);font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">Low</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral joint &amp; musculoskeletal injections</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Trigger point injections (incl. piriformis)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Sacroiliac joint injection &amp; sacral lateral branch blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Thoracic &amp; lumbar facet medial branch blocks &amp; RF denervation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve stimulation trial &amp; implant (location-dependent)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Pocket revision &amp; IPG placement</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--stone);margin:2px 0 16px\">In patients with elevated bleeding risk, reclassify intermediate-risk procedures as high risk and low-risk procedures as intermediate.</div>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--correct);margin-bottom:8px\">Low Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0 0 16px\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>Continue in most situations</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>Continue in most situations</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>Continue &mdash; ensure last dose taken &ge;6 hours before the procedure. Individual assessment if bleeding risk is high or INR &gt;3.0.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>Omit on day of procedure &mdash; allow &ge;2 half-lives since last dose (usually 24 hours). Longer if CrCl &lt;15 (drug accumulates unpredictably).</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>As other DOACs</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>48 hours</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A5200;margin-bottom:8px\">Intermediate Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0 0 16px\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Consider stopping if anatomically higher bleeding risk (e.g. cervical epidural, stellate ganglion block)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>7 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>5 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>5 days (target INR &le;1.2). Consider LMWH bridging if high thrombotic risk.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>3 days (5 half-lives)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>4 days (6 days if renal impairment)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--wrong);margin-bottom:8px\">High Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Primary prevention: 6 days<br>Secondary prevention: 4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>7 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>5 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>48 hours</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>5 days (target INR &le;1.2). Consider LMWH bridging if high thrombotic risk.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>3 days (5 half-lives)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>4 days (6 days if renal impairment)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_anticoag_warfarin_valve_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-08-10","rotationIndex":41,"question":"A 62-year-old man had a drug-eluting coronary stent inserted 6 weeks ago and remains on dual antiplatelet therapy (aspirin and clopidogrel). He requests a lumbar facet medial branch block for ongoing back pain. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Proceed with the block, continuing both antiplatelet agents","Stop clopidogrel for 7 days and proceed, continuing aspirin","Stop both agents for 7 days and proceed","Postpone the elective procedure and liaise with cardiology regarding timing","Proceed after bridging with LMWH"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<p>While lumbar medial branch blocks are a low-risk procedure, the key issue here is timing and dual antiplatelet therapy rather than procedure risk alone: this patient is still within the high-risk early window after drug-eluting stent insertion, when premature cessation of dual antiplatelet therapy carries a serious risk of stent thrombosis.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> While continuing a single antiplatelet is often reasonable for low-risk procedures in stable patients, this doesn''t address the more pressing issue of whether an elective procedure should proceed at 6 weeks post-stent in a patient on dual antiplatelet therapy.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B, C. Incorrect.</strong> Interrupting clopidogrel this early after a drug-eluting stent carries an unacceptable risk of stent thrombosis, a life-threatening complication.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Correct.</strong> The elective procedure should be deferred, with cardiology input on safe timing &mdash; this reflects the balance of thrombotic versus bleeding risk central to the guidance, prioritising avoidance of stent thrombosis over proceeding with an elective intervention.</p><p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Bridging is not indicated for antiplatelet agents, and doesn''t address the stent thrombosis risk of stopping clopidogrel.</p><div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Procedural Risk Stratification</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><table style=\"margin:0 0 6px\">\n    <tr><th>Risk Category</th><th>Example Procedures</th></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\"><td style=\"color:var(--wrong);font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">High</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Spinal cord stimulation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Intrathecal drug delivery systems</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Cervical transforaminal epidurals / nerve root blocks</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\"><td style=\"color:#7A5200;font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">Intermediate</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Interlaminar epidurals</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Thoracic &amp; lumbar transforaminal epidurals / nerve root blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Cervical facet medial branch blocks &amp; RF denervation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Sympathetic blocks (stellate, thoracic, splanchnic, coeliac, lumbar, hypogastric)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve stimulation trial &amp; implant (location-dependent)</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\"><td style=\"color:var(--correct);font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">Low</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral joint &amp; musculoskeletal injections</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Trigger point injections (incl. piriformis)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Sacroiliac joint injection &amp; sacral lateral branch blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Thoracic &amp; lumbar facet medial branch blocks &amp; RF denervation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve stimulation trial &amp; implant (location-dependent)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Pocket revision &amp; IPG placement</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--stone);margin:2px 0 16px\">In patients with elevated bleeding risk, reclassify intermediate-risk procedures as high risk and low-risk procedures as intermediate.</div>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--correct);margin-bottom:8px\">Low Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0 0 16px\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>Continue in most situations</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>Continue in most situations</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>Continue &mdash; ensure last dose taken &ge;6 hours before the procedure. Individual assessment if bleeding risk is high or INR &gt;3.0.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>Omit on day of procedure &mdash; allow &ge;2 half-lives since last dose (usually 24 hours). Longer if CrCl &lt;15 (drug accumulates unpredictably).</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>As other DOACs</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>48 hours</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A5200;margin-bottom:8px\">Intermediate Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0 0 16px\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Consider stopping if anatomically higher bleeding risk (e.g. cervical epidural, stellate ganglion block)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>7 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>5 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>5 days (target INR &le;1.2). Consider LMWH bridging if high thrombotic risk.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>3 days (5 half-lives)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>4 days (6 days if renal impairment)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--wrong);margin-bottom:8px\">High Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Primary prevention: 6 days<br>Secondary prevention: 4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>7 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>5 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>48 hours</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>5 days (target INR &le;1.2). Consider LMWH bridging if high thrombotic risk.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>3 days (5 half-lives)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>4 days (6 days if renal impairment)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_anticoag_dapt_stent_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-08-10","rotationIndex":43,"stem":"Stopping medications pre-lumbar medial branch block","lead":"For each scenario below, choose the single most appropriate management from the list above. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","options":["Continue medication unchanged","Discontinue 3 days before the procedure","Discontinue 5 days before the procedure","Discontinue 7 days before the procedure","Commence bridging therapy with therapeutic-dose LMWH","Normalise the INR before proceeding","Discontinue 24 hours before the procedure","Postpone the procedure"],"questions":[{"question":"A patient taking low-dose aspirin (75 mg) for secondary cardiovascular prevention is due to have a lumbar transforaminal epidural.","correct":0,"explanation":"<strong>A. Continue unchanged.</strong> Low-dose aspirin is not routinely stopped for intermediate-risk procedures; guidance supports a shared, individualised decision rather than blanket cessation."},{"question":"A patient taking apixaban for atrial fibrillation, with normal renal function, is listed for genicular blocks.","correct":0,"explanation":"<strong>A. Continue unchanged.</strong> Genicular blocks are low-risk procedures in an easily compressible area, and the risk of stopping anticoagulation here outweighs that of continuing. If the patient has multiple additional bleeding-risk factors, this can be considered on an individual basis."},{"question":"A patient taking clopidogrel following a bare-metal coronary stent inserted 10 years ago is listed for a spinal cord stimulator implant.","correct":3,"explanation":"<strong>D. Discontinue 7 days before.</strong> Clopidogrel''s irreversible effect means several days are needed for platelet turnover before a high-risk procedure; a bare-metal stent from a decade ago no longer carries an acute thrombosis risk that would preclude interruption."},{"question":"A patient on long-term warfarin for a mechanical heart valve requires an interlaminar cervical epidural.","correct":4,"explanation":"<strong>E. Bridge with therapeutic-dose LMWH.</strong> A mechanical valve places this patient in the high thromboembolic-risk category, for which bridging remains appropriate around a high-risk procedure, unlike routine AF."},{"question":"A patient taking a therapeutic dose of rivaroxaban for a pulmonary embolism sustained 3 weeks ago requires a sacroiliac joint injection.","correct":7,"explanation":"<strong>H. Postpone the procedure.</strong> A pulmonary embolism only 3 weeks earlier represents an acute, high thrombotic-risk period; interrupting therapeutic anticoagulation for an elective procedure at this stage is unsafe, so the intervention should be deferred rather than the anticoagulant interrupted."}],"reference":"<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Procedural Risk Stratification</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm);padding:16px 18px\">  <table style=\"margin:0 0 6px\">\n    <tr><th>Risk Category</th><th>Example Procedures</th></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\"><td style=\"color:var(--wrong);font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">High</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Spinal cord stimulation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Intrathecal drug delivery systems</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Cervical transforaminal epidurals / nerve root blocks</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\"><td style=\"color:#7A5200;font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">Intermediate</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Interlaminar epidurals</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Thoracic &amp; lumbar transforaminal epidurals / nerve root blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Cervical facet medial branch blocks &amp; RF denervation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Sympathetic blocks (stellate, thoracic, splanchnic, coeliac, lumbar, hypogastric)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve stimulation trial &amp; implant (location-dependent)</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n    <tr style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\"><td style=\"color:var(--correct);font-weight:700;vertical-align:top\">Low</td><td><ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:5px\"><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral joint &amp; musculoskeletal injections</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Trigger point injections (incl. piriformis)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Sacroiliac joint injection &amp; sacral lateral branch blocks</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Thoracic &amp; lumbar facet medial branch blocks &amp; RF denervation</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Peripheral nerve stimulation trial &amp; implant (location-dependent)</span></li><li style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:8px;line-height:1.5\"><span style=\"width:4px;height:4px;border-radius:50%;background:#A99E95;margin-top:7px;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block\"></span><span>Pocket revision &amp; IPG placement</span></li></ul></td></tr>\n  </table>\n  <div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--stone);margin:2px 0 16px\">In patients with elevated bleeding risk, reclassify intermediate-risk procedures as high risk and low-risk procedures as intermediate.</div>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--correct);margin-bottom:8px\">Low Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0 0 16px\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:var(--correct-bg)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>Continue in most situations</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>Continue in most situations</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>Continue &mdash; ensure last dose taken &ge;6 hours before the procedure. Individual assessment if bleeding risk is high or INR &gt;3.0.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>Omit on day of procedure &mdash; allow &ge;2 half-lives since last dose (usually 24 hours). Longer if CrCl &lt;15 (drug accumulates unpredictably).</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>As other DOACs</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>48 hours</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A5200;margin-bottom:8px\">Intermediate Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0 0 16px\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:rgba(245,165,36,.11)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Consider stopping if anatomically higher bleeding risk (e.g. cervical epidural, stellate ganglion block)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>7 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>5 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>Continue</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>5 days (target INR &le;1.2). Consider LMWH bridging if high thrombotic risk.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>3 days (5 half-lives)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>4 days (6 days if renal impairment)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table>\n\n  <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--wrong);margin-bottom:8px\">High Risk Procedure &mdash; Stop / Restart</div>\n  <table style=\"margin:0\">\n    <tr><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Drug</th><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Stop</th><th style=\"background:var(--wrong-bg)\">Restart</th></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Primary prevention: 6 days<br>Secondary prevention: 4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Clopidogrel / Prasugrel</td><td>7 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Ticagrelor</td><td>5 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dipyridamole</td><td>48 hours</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Warfarin</td><td>5 days (target INR &le;1.2). Consider LMWH bridging if high thrombotic risk.</td><td>&ge;6 hours (ideally 24 hours)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>DOACs (except dabigatran)</td><td>3 days (5 half-lives)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>4 days (6 days if renal impairment)</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>IV Heparin</td><td>6 hours pre-procedure</td><td>2 hours (low-bleeding risk), 24 hours (high-bleeding risk)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (prophylactic)</td><td>12 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>LMWH (therapeutic)</td><td>24 hours</td><td>12&ndash;24 hours</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>4 days</td><td>24 hours</td></tr>\n  </table></div>\n</div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_emq_anticoag_lmbb_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":44,"stem":"Receptors and mechanisms of action of pain therapies","lead":"For each scenario below, select the single best-matching answer from the list above. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","options":["Mu receptor","Delta receptor","Acetylcholine receptor","Alpha-2-delta subunit","GABA receptor","Sodium channel","TRPV1 receptor","NMDA receptor"],"questions":[{"question":"Conventional TENS (high-frequency, low-intensity), used for analgesia.","correct":1,"explanation":"<strong>B. Delta receptor</strong> — Conventional (high-frequency, low-intensity) TENS is largely gate-control mediated and acts predominantly through spinal delta-opioid receptor activation, distinguishing it from acupuncture-like TENS."},{"question":"Acupuncture-like TENS (low-frequency, high-intensity), used for analgesia.","correct":0,"explanation":"<strong>A. Mu receptor</strong> — Low-frequency, high-intensity acupuncture-like TENS triggers release of endogenous opioids acting at mu-opioid receptors, and its effect is more reliably reversed by naloxone than that of conventional TENS."},{"question":"Gabapentin, used for neuropathic pain.","correct":3,"explanation":"<strong>D. Alpha-2-delta subunit</strong> — Despite the name, gabapentin (and pregabalin) do not act on GABA receptors. They bind the alpha-2-delta subunit of presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels, reducing calcium influx and downstream release of excitatory neurotransmitters (glutamate, substance P) in the dorsal horn."},{"question":"Lamotrigine, used for trigeminal neuralgia.","correct":5,"explanation":"<strong>F. Sodium channel</strong> — Lamotrigine, like carbamazepine and other membrane-stabilising anticonvulsants used in trigeminal neuralgia, works primarily by blocking voltage-gated sodium channels, stabilising their inactive state and reducing ectopic neuronal firing."},{"question":"Capsaicin 8% patch (Qutenza), used for peripheral neuropathic pain.","correct":6,"explanation":"<strong>G. TRPV1 receptor</strong> — Capsaicin 8% is a TRPV1 agonist; sustained receptor activation causes calcium influx and subsequent reversible &ldquo;defunctionalisation&rdquo; of cutaneous nociceptors, producing prolonged analgesia after a single application.<p style=\"color:#7C7068;font-size:13px;margin-top:14px\">Acetylcholine receptors (C), GABA receptors (E), and NMDA receptors (H) are not the primary mechanism for any of the five scenarios and remain unused in this set.</p>"}]}'::jsonb where id = 'q_emq_receptor_moa_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":45,"stem":"Paediatric pain assessment scales","lead":"For each of the following scenarios, select the most likely choice from the list of options above. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","options":["CRIES scale","COMFORT scale","Paediatric Pain Profile (PPP)","Functional Disability Inventory (FDI)","FLACC scale","Faces scale","Paediatric Quality of Life Inventory (PedsQL)","Visual Analogue Scale (VAS)"],"questions":[{"question":"35-week neonate, post-inguinal hernia repair.","correct":0,"explanation":"<strong>A. CRIES scale</strong> — CRIES (Crying, Requires oxygen, Increased vital signs, Expression, Sleeplessness) is a behavioural scale validated for acute postoperative pain in neonates from around 32 weeks&rsquo; gestation, making it appropriate for this 35-week neonate."},{"question":"12-year-old with back pain and severe cognitive dysfunction.","correct":2,"explanation":"<strong>C. Paediatric Pain Profile (PPP)</strong> — The PPP is a 20-item behaviour-rating scale validated for assessing pain in children with severe to profound neurological impairment. It is designed for use in the home setting by a parent, as well as in the hospital or hospice setting by health professionals."},{"question":"6-year-old having venepuncture.","correct":5,"explanation":"<strong>F. Faces scale</strong> — The Faces scale is a self-report tool suited to school-age children old enough to identify with facial expressions of pain, appropriate for a cooperative 6-year-old facing a brief procedural pain event.\n<div style=\"margin-top:14px;text-align:center\">\n  <img src=\"images/faces-pain-scale.jpg\" alt=\"Faces Pain Scale — Revised, showing six faces from 0 (no pain) to 5 (worst pain)\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;border:1px solid #EDE3D7;background:#FFFAF4;padding:14px 10px\">\n</div>"},{"question":"3-year-old on day 1 post emergency appendicectomy.","correct":4,"explanation":"<strong>E. FLACC scale</strong> — FLACC (Face, Legs, Activity, Cry, Consolability) is a behavioural observational scale validated for infants and young children unable to reliably self-report, fitting the postoperative 3-year-old. The revised FLACC tool identifies specific indicators of pain in children with severe cognitive impairment, including spasms, self-harm behaviours, consoling actions, and breathing or verbal noises.\n<table style=\"margin-top:14px\">\n  <tr><th>Category</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Face</strong></td><td>No particular expression or smile</td><td>Occasional grimace or frown, withdrawn, disinterested</td><td>Frequent to constant frown, quivering chin, clenched jaw</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Legs</strong></td><td>Normal position or relaxed</td><td>Uneasy, restless, tense</td><td>Kicking or legs drawn up</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Activity</strong></td><td>Lying quietly, normal position, moves easily</td><td>Squirming, shifting back and forth, tense</td><td>Arched, rigid or jerking</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Cry</strong></td><td>No cry (awake or asleep)</td><td>Moans or whimpers, occasional complaint</td><td>Crying steadily, screams or sobs, frequent complaints</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Consolability</strong></td><td>Content, relaxed</td><td>Reassured by occasional touching, hugging or being talked to, distractible</td><td>Difficult to console or comfort</td></tr>\n</table>"},{"question":"9-year-old with juvenile idiopathic arthritis, being reviewed in a chronic pain clinic.","correct":6,"explanation":"<strong>G. Paediatric Quality of Life Inventory (PedsQL)</strong> — The PedsQL is a multiple-domain tool that broadly assesses function in children with chronic pain, fitting a child being followed longitudinally in a chronic pain clinic rather than being scored for acute pain intensity. Three age-banded versions exist (young child 5&ndash;7 years, child 8&ndash;12 years, teen 13&ndash;18 years), each measuring four functional subdomains &mdash; physical, emotional, social, and school &mdash; to produce a scale summary score, physical health score, and psychosocial health score. Higher scores indicate better function, and analogous carer-proxy versions allow comparison between patient- and carer-reported function."}],"reference":"<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Unselected Options — Key Definitions</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><table style=\"margin:0\">\n  <tr><th>Scale</th><th>Definition</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>COMFORT</strong></td><td>Designed to assess distress in ventilated patients: 6 behavioural items (alertness, calmness, muscle tone, movement, facial tension, respiratory response) plus 2 physiological items (heart rate, mean arterial pressure). The COMFORT-B revision removed the physiological items and added crying as a behavioural item for non-ventilated children. Takes ~3 minutes; used from 0&ndash;17 years, including unconscious or sedated patients.</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Functional Disability Inventory (FDI)</strong></td><td>Self/parent-report measure of the impact of pain on physical and functional activities of daily living, used mainly in chronic paediatric pain.</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Visual Analogue Scale (VAS)</strong></td><td>A 10cm (100mm) line anchored &lsquo;no pain&rsquo; to &lsquo;worst pain imaginable&rsquo;; the patient marks the line, and the score is read as the distance from zero (0&ndash;10 or 0&ndash;100 scale). Requires abstract/cognitive maturity, typically usable from ~7&ndash;8 years upward.\n    <div style=\"margin-top:10px\"><img src=\"images/vas-scale.svg\" alt=\"Visual Analogue Scale — a 10cm line anchored from no pain to worst pain imaginable\" style=\"max-width:100%\"></div>\n  </td></tr>\n</table></div></div>\n</div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_emq_paediatric_pain_scales_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":46,"stem":"Nerves of the lower limb","lead":"Select the single most likely nerve for each scenario. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","options":["Tibial nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Saphenous nerve","Obturator nerve","Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (LFCN)"],"questions":[{"question":"Loss of inversion and plantar flexion; lateral foot sensory loss.","correct":0,"explanation":"<strong>A. Tibial nerve</strong> — The tibial nerve supplies tibialis posterior (inversion) and gastrocnemius/soleus (plantar flexion); its lateral plantar branch supplies the lateral sole, accounting for the lateral sensory loss."},{"question":"Loss of all function below the knee following hip surgery, with medial leg/foot sensation retained.","correct":4,"explanation":"<strong>E. Sciatic nerve</strong> — Sciatic nerve injury is a recognised complication of hip surgery (retraction or stretch, especially via the posterior approach). It supplies everything below the knee via its tibial and common peroneal divisions, while medial leg/foot sensation is preserved as it arises from the saphenous nerve (a femoral branch), outside the sciatic distribution."},{"question":"Loss of eversion and dorsiflexion.","correct":1,"explanation":"<strong>B. Common peroneal nerve</strong> — The common peroneal nerve divides into the superficial peroneal (eversion, peroneus longus/brevis) and deep peroneal (dorsiflexion, tibialis anterior) branches; injury causes the classic foot drop."},{"question":"Loss of adduction; sensory loss over the medial thigh.","correct":6,"explanation":"<strong>G. Obturator nerve</strong> — The obturator nerve supplies the hip adductors and provides sensation over the medial thigh."},{"question":"Sensory loss over the anterolateral thigh following inguinal hernia repair, in a patient with a high BMI.","correct":7,"explanation":"<strong>H. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (LFCN)</strong> — The LFCN is a purely sensory nerve to the anterolateral thigh; meralgia paraesthetica from LFCN compression or injury is associated with high BMI and with inguinal/pelvic surgery due to its course near the inguinal ligament."}],"reference":"<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Lower Limb Nerve — Key Definitions</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><table style=\"margin:0\">\n  <tr><th>Nerve</th><th>Definition</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (LFCN)</strong></td><td>A purely sensory nerve to the anterolateral thigh; meralgia paraesthetica from LFCN compression or injury is associated with high BMI and with inguinal/pelvic surgery due to its course near the inguinal ligament.</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Sural nerve</strong></td><td>Purely sensory (tibial + common peroneal contributions), supplying the posterolateral leg and lateral foot/little toe; no motor component, often used as a nerve graft donor.</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Femoral nerve</strong></td><td>Motor to iliacus, sartorius, pectineus and quadriceps femoris; sensory to the anterior thigh, with its saphenous branch extending to the medial leg/foot.</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Saphenous nerve</strong></td><td>Purely sensory terminal branch of the femoral nerve, supplying the medial leg and medial foot to the medial malleolus/first metatarsal region; no motor function.</td></tr>\n</table></div></div>\n</div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_emq_lower_limb_nerves_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":47,"stem":"Opioid addiction and related phenomena","lead":"Select the single most likely term for each scenario. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","options":["Addiction","Pseudoaddiction","Drug diversion","Opioid-induced hyperalgesia (OIH)","Tolerance","Generalised anxiety disorder","Opioid toxicity","Dependence"],"questions":[{"question":"A chronic pain patient describes that opioids help with pain relief, but says he also enjoys the &ldquo;high&rdquo;, and reports snorting and injecting his tablets.","correct":0,"explanation":"<strong>A. Addiction</strong> — Addiction is a behavioural disorder characterised by the &ldquo;4 Cs&rdquo;: craving, compulsive use, loss of control, and continued use despite harm. Enjoyment of the psychoactive &ldquo;high&rdquo; and use via non-prescribed routes (snorting, injecting) are hallmark features distinguishing this from therapeutic opioid use."},{"question":"An elderly patient on long-term opioids presents with myoclonus, irritability, and diarrhoea.","correct":7,"explanation":"<strong>H. Dependence</strong> — Myoclonus, irritability, and diarrhoea together represent a withdrawal picture — the physical manifestation of dependence, a physiological state of neuroadaptation that produces tolerance and, if the drug is abruptly stopped or reduced, physical withdrawal symptoms."},{"question":"A patient admitted with an L3 compression fracture has her opioid changed from modified-release morphine 30mg BD to immediate-release morphine 20mg 3-hourly. She keeps requesting analgesia frequently and becomes agitated when morphine is found in her cabinet.","correct":1,"explanation":"<strong>B. Pseudoaddiction</strong> — Pseudoaddiction describes drug-seeking behaviour (frequent requests, agitation, distress) arising from undertreated pain rather than true addiction; it resolves once analgesia is adequately titrated, unlike genuine addiction."},{"question":"A patient with chronic widespread pain reports that her pain worsens after taking opioids.","correct":3,"explanation":"<strong>D. Opioid-induced hyperalgesia (OIH)</strong> — OIH is a paradoxical state in which opioid exposure sensitises pain pathways, producing worsening and more widespread pain despite (or because of) increasing opioid dose — the opposite of an analgesic response."},{"question":"A patient with multiple sclerosis has escalating opioid requests to her GP, who notices that her husband — her main carer — has undergone a change in behaviour.","correct":2,"explanation":"<strong>C. Drug diversion</strong> — Drug diversion refers to a third party (here, the carer) redirecting the patient&rsquo;s prescribed opioids for their own use or sale; a carer&rsquo;s behavioural change alongside the patient&rsquo;s rising analgesia requirements should raise this suspicion."}],"reference":"<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Opioid Phenomena — Key Definitions</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px\"><table style=\"margin:0\">\n  <tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Drug diversion</strong></td><td>Drug diversion refers to a third party (here, the carer) redirecting the patient&rsquo;s prescribed opioids for their own use or sale; a carer&rsquo;s behavioural change alongside the patient&rsquo;s rising analgesia requirements should raise this suspicion.</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Tolerance</strong></td><td>A pharmacological phenomenon where increasing doses are required to achieve the same analgesic effect over time, due to receptor-level neuroadaptation; expected with chronic opioid use and not itself evidence of addiction.</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Generalised anxiety disorder</strong></td><td>Persistent, excessive worry about multiple life domains for &ge;6 months with somatic symptoms (restlessness, muscle tension, sleep disturbance); distinct from opioid-related phenomena, though it can coexist with and complicate chronic pain management.</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Opioid toxicity</strong></td><td>Accumulation of opioid/active metabolites (e.g. in renal impairment) causing sedation, respiratory depression, pinpoint pupils and myoclonus; unlike withdrawal, it typically causes constipation rather than diarrhoea, with progressive CNS/respiratory depression.</td></tr>\n</table></div></div>\n</div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_emq_opioid_addiction_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":48,"stem":"Migraine subtypes","lead":"Select the single most likely diagnosis for each scenario. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all.","options":["Hemiplegic migraine","Migraine with aura","Migraine without aura","Vestibular migraine","Brainstem migraine","Chronic migraine","Medication overuse headache","Tension-type headache"],"questions":[{"question":"A 28-year-old woman has recurrent headaches preceded by 20 minutes of visual zigzag lines and scotoma, followed by a throbbing unilateral headache with photophobia lasting several hours.","correct":1,"explanation":"<strong>B. Migraine with aura</strong> — Migraine with aura is characterised by fully reversible visual, sensory, or speech symptoms (here, a typical visual aura of zigzag lines/scotoma) preceding or accompanying the headache."},{"question":"A 34-year-old man has episodic vertigo lasting hours, associated with headache and nausea; no vestibular signs are found between episodes, and MRI is normal.","correct":3,"explanation":"<strong>D. Vestibular migraine</strong> — Vestibular migraine presents with episodic vertigo, often with associated headache/nausea, in a patient with a migraine history; interictal vestibular examination and imaging are typically normal."},{"question":"A 45-year-old woman using triptans and codeine on most days for headache now describes near-daily headache that has become less responsive to her usual medications.","correct":6,"explanation":"<strong>G. Medication overuse headache</strong> — Defined as headache that developed or worsened while taking, for 3 months or more: triptans, opioids, ergots or combination analgesic medications on 10 or more days per month; or paracetamol, aspirin or an NSAID (alone or in combination) on 15 or more days per month."},{"question":"A 22-year-old woman has an episode of unilateral limb weakness lasting an hour, followed by headache; her mother had similar episodes.","correct":0,"explanation":"<strong>A. Hemiplegic migraine</strong> — Hemiplegic migraine is a rare subtype of migraine with aura featuring motor weakness as part of the aura, often with a family history given its association with autosomal dominant genetic mutations (e.g. CACNA1A)."},{"question":"A 50-year-old woman has had headache on 20 days per month for the past 4 months.","correct":5,"explanation":"<strong>F. Chronic migraine</strong> — Chronic migraine is defined as headache on &ge;15 days per month for more than 3 months, with features of migraine present on at least some of those days."}],"reference":"<div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Migraine — Background & Diagnostic Criteria</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm)\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px\"><p style=\"margin:0\">Migraine is a primary headache disorder characterised by recurrent attacks of moderate-to-severe headache, typically unilateral and pulsating, associated with autonomic and sensory features (nausea, photophobia, phonophobia), and in some patients preceded or accompanied by transient, fully reversible neurological symptoms known as aura. It is thought to arise from a combination of neuronal hyperexcitability (cortical spreading depression, particularly relevant to aura) and activation of the trigeminovascular system, leading to release of vasoactive neuropeptides (e.g. CGRP) and central sensitisation.</p></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Episodic vs Chronic Migraine</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Episodic migraine</strong></td><td>Headache occurring on fewer than 15 days per month</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Chronic migraine</strong></td><td>Headache occurring on &ge;15 days per month for &gt;3 months, with features of migraine</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Diagnostic Criteria &mdash; Migraine Without Aura</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Component</th><th>Requirement</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Attack frequency</strong></td><td>At least 5 attacks fulfilling all criteria below</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Duration</strong></td><td>4&ndash;72 hours (untreated or unsuccessfully treated)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Headache features (&ge;2 required)</strong></td><td>Unilateral; pulsating quality; moderate to severe intensity; exacerbated by routine physical activity</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Associated features (&ge;1 required)</strong></td><td>Nausea and/or vomiting; photophobia or phonophobia</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 4px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong)\"><div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">Diagnostic Criteria &mdash; Migraine With Aura</div><table style=\"margin:0\"><tr><th>Component</th><th>Requirement</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Attack frequency</strong></td><td>At least 2 attacks associated with aura symptoms</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Aura symptom type (&ge;1 required)</strong></td><td>Visual; sensory; speech or language; motor; brainstem; retinal</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Aura characteristics (&ge;3 required)</strong></td><td>Gradual spread over &ge;5 min for at least one symptom; stepwise, successive onset of aura symptoms; each symptom resolves within 60 min; at least one unilateral symptom; at least one positive symptom; headache occurs during aura, or within 60 min of aura onset</td></tr></table></div></div>\n</div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_emq_migraine_subtypes_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":49,"question":"A 46-year-old office worker presents with a 4-month history of right-sided low back pain radiating into the buttock, worse on sitting and rising from sitting. Examination reveals a weakly positive piriformis test; sacroiliac joint provocation testing is negative. Lumbar spine MRI is normal. She wants to engage with physiotherapy, but reports pain is limiting her participation. What is the most appropriate next step to facilitate physiotherapy?","choices":["Caudal epidural steroid injection","Sacroiliac joint injection","Diagnostic medial branch block","Piriformis injection","Oral steroid course"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<p>This vignette describes a patient in whom SIJ and lumbar discogenic/radicular causes have been reasonably excluded (negative provocation testing, normal MRI). While the piriformis test (FAIR test) is weakly positive, this is not highly diagnostically accurate. Mechanical, unilateral pain with a radicular flavour that does not extend beyond the knee should prompt exclusion of facet joint pathology via a diagnostic, local-anaesthetic-only medial branch block.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Caudal epidural steroid injection has low diagnostic value and provides no future strategy for potential radiofrequency ablation; it is better suited to lumbosacral radicular pain or foraminal stenosis.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> SIJ provocation testing is explicitly negative here, making SIJ-mediated pain unlikely and this option not the best next step.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Correct.</strong> Diagnostic medial branch block targets the lumbar facet joints and provides excellent diagnostic value for suitability of radiofrequency denervation and long-lasting analgesia. Facet joint pain is classically mechanical (worse on extension, lateral rotation, and change of position) and can frequently refer to other areas through mechanical irritation of nearby roots via facet joint hypertrophy, or chemical irritation from inflammation of bony structures.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Piriformis syndrome — a form of &ldquo;deep gluteal syndrome&rdquo; caused by sciatic nerve irritation as it passes through or near the piriformis — is a recognised but controversial cause of buttock pain. However, the FAIR test&rsquo;s weak positivity and low diagnostic accuracy make a piriformis injection premature before facet pathology has been excluded.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Systemic steroids are not first-line for localised musculoskeletal pain and lack the targeted precision of an image-guided injection directed at the suspected pain generator.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">RF Denervation &amp; the FAIR Test</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm);\"><div style=\"padding:16px 18px 14px\">\n    <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--coral-deep);margin-bottom:8px\">NICE Guidance &mdash; Radiofrequency (RF) Denervation</div>\n    <table style=\"margin:0\">\n    <tr><th>Component</th><th>Requirement</th></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Indication for referral</strong></td><td>Non-surgical treatment has not worked, <strong>and</strong> the main source of pain is thought to come from structures supplied by the medial branch nerve, <strong>and</strong> the patient has moderate or severe localised back pain (&ge;5 on a VAS or equivalent) at the time of referral</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Prerequisite</strong></td><td>Only perform RF denervation after a <strong>positive response to a diagnostic medial branch block</strong></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Imaging</strong></td><td>Do <strong>not</strong> offer imaging for specific facet joint pain as a prerequisite for RF denervation</td></tr>\n  </table>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"padding:16px 18px 4px;border-top:2px solid var(--border-strong)\">\n    <div style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--stone);margin-bottom:8px\">FAIR Test (Flexion, Adduction, Internal Rotation)</div>\n    <p style=\"margin:0;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\">A provocation test for piriformis syndrome. The patient lies supine (or lateral, hip being tested uppermost); the hip is passively flexed to ~60&ndash;90&deg;, adducted across the midline, and internally rotated, stretching/compressing the piriformis against the sciatic nerve. A positive test reproduces the patient&rsquo;s typical buttock pain, sometimes radiating down the posterior thigh.</p>\n  </div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_sba_piriformis_facet_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":50,"question":"A 58-year-old man with a history of alcohol excess, ischaemic heart disease, and type 2 diabetes mellitus presents shortly after hospital discharge with an acutely painful lump over his right great toe. On examination, the lump is chalky-white in colour, irregular in shape, hot, firm, and exquisitely tender. There is no history of trauma. What is the most appropriate investigation to confirm the diagnosis?","choices":["Erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR)","Skin biopsy","Synovial fluid aspiration with polarised light microscopy","Serum uric acid level","Toe X-ray"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<p>This vignette describes a gouty tophus with likely acute-on-chronic gout, in a patient with classic risk factors (alcohol excess, metabolic syndrome/T2DM, cardiovascular disease, and recent physiological stress from hospital admission). The chalky, irregular, firm lump is characteristic of a tophus. While NICE guidance suggests measuring urate levels to support diagnosis, this question asks for the <strong>best diagnostic modality</strong> — which is synovial fluid aspiration demonstrating monosodium urate crystals.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> ESR is a nonspecific inflammatory marker that may be raised in acute gout (or many other inflammatory/infective conditions), but does not confirm the diagnosis or distinguish gout from other causes such as septic arthritis or pseudogout.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Skin biopsy could histologically demonstrate urate crystal deposition within a tophus, but this is more invasive, slower, and less specific/practical than joint aspiration; it is not the standard first-line diagnostic test.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Correct.</strong> The gold-standard investigation for gout is aspiration of joint/tophus fluid with polarised light microscopy, demonstrating needle-shaped, negatively birefringent monosodium urate crystals. This also allows exclusion of septic arthritis via Gram stain and culture — a crucial differential given the hot, firm, tender presentation.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> NICE NG219 suggests measuring serum urate to confirm the diagnosis, and this would be the correct answer if the question asked for the best <em>next step</em> rather than the best diagnostic <em>modality</em>, which is synovial fluid analysis.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Toe X-ray may show established changes of chronic tophaceous gout (juxta-articular erosions with overhanging edges) but is neither sensitive nor specific for an acute presentation and does not provide a definitive diagnosis.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"nice-guideline-btn\" onclick=\"openGuidelineModal(''ng219'',''NICE NG219 — Gout'')\">\n  <svg width=\"15\" height=\"15\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><path d=\"M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z\"/><polyline points=\"14 2 14 8 20 8\"/><line x1=\"9\" y1=\"13\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"13\"/><line x1=\"9\" y1=\"17\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"17\"/></svg>\n  NICE NG219 &mdash; Gout Summary\n</button></div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_sba_gout_diagnosis_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":51,"question":"A 55-year-old man presents with an acute flare of pain affecting his first metatarsophalangeal joint. He has no history of renal impairment, peptic ulcer disease, or cardiovascular contraindications. He has no known drug allergies. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment for this acute flare?","choices":["Paracetamol","Opioid analgesia","Etoricoxib","Topical NSAID gel","Intra-articular steroid injection"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<p>Acute gout flares are driven by an intense inflammatory response to monosodium urate crystal deposition. First-line management centres on rapid, effective anti-inflammatory treatment — NSAIDs (including COX-2 selective agents), colchicine, or a short course of oral corticosteroids — chosen according to comorbidities and contraindications.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Paracetamol is a simple analgesic with no significant anti-inflammatory action; it is inadequate as monotherapy for the intense inflammatory pain of an acute gout flare.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> Opioid analgesia addresses pain but does nothing to treat the underlying crystal-induced inflammation, and carries unnecessary dependence/side-effect risk when effective anti-inflammatory options exist and are not contraindicated.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Correct.</strong> Etoricoxib is a COX-2 selective NSAID specifically licensed for acute gout, providing effective anti-inflammatory analgesia. In a patient without renal, cardiovascular, or GI contraindications, an oral NSAID/COX-2 inhibitor is considered first-line treatment.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> Topical NSAID gel provides insufficient systemic anti-inflammatory effect for an acute, intensely inflamed joint; it is better suited to superficial musculoskeletal pain than true crystal arthropathy.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Intra-articular steroid injection is effective, particularly for monoarticular flares or when oral options are contraindicated, but requires exclusion of septic arthritis first and is generally reserved for cases where oral therapy is unsuitable or the diagnosis is already confirmed — making it not the best first-line choice over an oral NSAID in this straightforward scenario.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"nice-guideline-btn\" onclick=\"openGuidelineModal(''ng219'',''NICE NG219 — Gout'')\">\n  <svg width=\"15\" height=\"15\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><path d=\"M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z\"/><polyline points=\"14 2 14 8 20 8\"/><line x1=\"9\" y1=\"13\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"13\"/><line x1=\"9\" y1=\"17\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"17\"/></svg>\n  NICE NG219 &mdash; Gout Summary\n</button></div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_sba_gout_flare_treatment_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":52,"question":"A 12-year-old boy, previously treated for Langerhans cell histiocytosis, presents with an ongoing history of low back pain. MRI demonstrates a possible pars interarticularis defect at L4/L5, without associated spondylolisthesis. His pain has become increasingly disabling, and he has started refusing to attend school. There are no red flag features (no neurological deficit, no night pain, no systemic symptoms of relapse). What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Referral to a complex spine multidisciplinary team (MDT)","Referral to a clinical psychologist","Trial of stronger analgesia (opioid escalation)","Referral for a spinal surgical opinion regarding the pars defect","Referral to physiotherapy"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<p>This vignette illustrates a mismatch between a relatively minor structural finding (an isolated pars defect without slip) and a significant functional impact (school refusal) — a classic marker that psychosocial factors, rather than the structural lesion alone, are driving disability. In a child with a prior cancer diagnosis, school avoidance disproportionate to imaging findings should prompt psychological assessment as the priority, since untreated psychological/behavioural drivers (health anxiety, fear-avoidance, family anxiety around symptoms after a cancer history) will undermine any purely physical treatment approach.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> A complex spine MDT referral would be appropriate if the pars defect were felt to be a significant structural driver requiring surgical/bracing consideration (e.g. with spondylolisthesis, neurological signs, or refractory mechanical pain), but the absence of slip or red flags makes a spine-focused referral too narrow given the dominant functional/psychosocial picture.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Correct.</strong> Given the degree of functional impairment (school refusal) out of proportion to the structural finding, and the psychologically vulnerable context of prior cancer treatment, referral to psychology is the most appropriate next step to address the primary driver of disability.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Escalating opioids in paediatric musculoskeletal pain without addressing functional/psychological drivers is inappropriate, risks medicalising a functional pain state, and carries dependence/misuse risk with limited benefit.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Incorrect.</strong> A pars defect without spondylolisthesis is generally managed conservatively; surgical referral is not indicated in the absence of instability or neurological compromise.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> Physiotherapy is a reasonable component of overall management, but treating the physical symptom alone without addressing the marked functional/psychological impairment (school refusal) is unlikely to resolve the presentation, and is not the best next step compared with targeting the dominant issue directly.</p>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_sba_paeds_psychology_pars_001';
update questions set data = '{"dateAdded":"2026-09-02","rotationIndex":53,"question":"A 3-year-old girl with acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (ALL) develops severe oral mucositis following chemotherapy. What is the most appropriate treatment for her pain?","choices":["Oral morphine","Rectal ketorolac","Topical lidocaine mouthwash","IV nurse-controlled analgesia (NCA)","Bolus of intravenous opioid"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<p>Severe mucositis in a young child causes intense, continuous pain with an inability to tolerate oral intake (due to mucosal breakdown and swallowing difficulty). In this age group, formal patient-controlled analgesia (PCA) is not feasible, making nurse-controlled analgesia (NCA) — where a trained nurse administers bolus doses of IV opioid according to a preset protocol, based on behavioural pain assessment — the appropriate route and mode of delivery for reliable, titratable, and rapid analgesia.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:12px\"><strong>A. Incorrect.</strong> Oral intake is likely to be poorly tolerated or unreliable given severe mucosal breakdown and pain on swallowing, making the oral route unsuitable and absorption unpredictable.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>B. Incorrect.</strong> NSAIDs are relatively contraindicated in this setting due to the risk of chemotherapy-associated thrombocytopenia and bleeding from friable, ulcerated mucosa; ketorolac in particular carries a significant bleeding-risk profile.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>C. Incorrect.</strong> Topical lidocaine mouthwash may provide some adjunctive symptomatic relief but is inadequate as sole treatment for severe, diffuse mucositis pain, and carries a risk of systemic absorption/toxicity with extensive mucosal ulceration, plus a risk of aspiration if oral sensation is significantly numbed in a young child.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>D. Correct.</strong> IV nurse-controlled analgesia allows titratable, rapid opioid delivery with nurse oversight, appropriate for a 3-year-old who cannot manage a self-administered PCA device, while allowing careful monitoring for sedation/respiratory depression.</p>\n<p style=\"margin-top:8px\"><strong>E. Incorrect.</strong> A bolus of intravenous opioid may be a suitable initial therapy, but it is not the <em>most</em> appropriate treatment, which would be ongoing analgesia via nurse-led NCA rather than a single bolus.</p>\n<div style=\"margin-top:18px\"><div style=\"border:1px solid var(--border-strong);border-radius:14px;overflow:hidden\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:12px 18px;background:var(--coral)\">\n    <span style=\"font-weight:700;color:#fff;font-size:14.5px\">Oral Mucositis</span>\n    <div style=\"display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-shrink:0\">\n      <svg width=\"14\" height=\"14\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"12\"/><line x1=\"12\" y1=\"8\" x2=\"12.01\" y2=\"8\"/></svg>\n      <span style=\"font-family:var(--mono);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#fff;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.55);border-radius:999px;padding:3px 9px\">Knowledge</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:var(--surface-warm);\"><div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:12px;padding:11px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(237,227,215,.6);font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><span style=\"width:6px;height:6px;border-radius:50%;margin-top:8px;flex-shrink:0;background:var(--stone-light)\"></span><span>Oral mucositis (OM) is a common side effect of chemotherapy or radiotherapy used for treating head and neck cancer, or before bone marrow transplantation.</span></div><div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:12px;padding:11px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(237,227,215,.6);font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><span style=\"width:6px;height:6px;border-radius:50%;margin-top:8px;flex-shrink:0;background:var(--stone-light)\"></span><span>Symptoms usually start 5&ndash;10 days after chemotherapy, or 14 days after radiotherapy, and include dryness, halitosis, pain, inflammation and oral mucosal ulceration.</span></div><div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:12px;padding:11px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(237,227,215,.6);font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><span style=\"width:6px;height:6px;border-radius:50%;margin-top:8px;flex-shrink:0;background:var(--stone-light)\"></span><span>Chemotherapy-associated OM can resolve within a few days of completing chemotherapy, but radiotherapy-associated OM can last for weeks.</span></div><div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:12px;padding:11px 18px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(237,227,215,.6);font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><span style=\"width:6px;height:6px;border-radius:50%;margin-top:8px;flex-shrink:0;background:var(--stone-light)\"></span><span>OM can affect nutritional status (sometimes requiring enteral or parenteral nutrition) and quality of life, and can increase hospital stay. It can also require interruptions or dose reductions in chemotherapy or radiotherapy.</span></div><div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:12px;padding:11px 18px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink-soft)\"><span style=\"width:6px;height:6px;border-radius:50%;margin-top:8px;flex-shrink:0;background:var(--stone-light)\"></span><span><strong>Current treatments:</strong> comprehensive oral hygiene, good hydration, a bland soft diet, and avoiding alcohol and tobacco may increase comfort. Ice, water-based moisturisers, painkillers and NSAIDs can help reduce symptoms. Drugs such as palifermin are sometimes used to prevent or treat OM, and antibiotics may be needed for infectious complications. Low-level laser therapy may also be used to prevent or treat OM.</span></div></div>\n</div></div>"}'::jsonb where id = 'q_sba_paeds_mucositis_001';
